on the fret side of things we can't take  our eyes off of the regional crises  which are in still in full flow actually  in the neighboring regions of europe i  think primarily of the sahel but i also  think of the horn of africa and i also  think of syria  it forms in a sense a regional arc  around europe of instability  which europe has to get to grips with it  has started to do so  but it's rather a long-term project  involving of course the security and  defense side of things but also  development issues come into play  diplomacy economic development and so  forth so um really we're sitting in a  sea of instability as it were on our on  our doorstep  and they're the the main threats that  face us of course there are challenges  um one can't really call them threats as  such but there are challenges of course  emanating from  from the east one thinks of the rise of  china which in itself isn't a threat but  of course may cause security challenges  in asia which in turn may have knock-on  effects for europe  so  of course europe's security panorama as  it were or vision has to be global even  if a lot of the threats are on its  doorstep  if you take the case of mali for example  you had a number of crises come together  the first one being of course terrorism  a terrorist threat which we know in  europe does affect us directly one  needn't think further than the madrid  bombings or the london bombings to see  this but on top of that you have a  number of layered issues one can think  of transnational  crime networks of drug smuggling and so  forth which comes through the region  which also in turns impacts impact the  you know europe um  i think on top of that um one must also  think a little bit about the spiller  spillover effects of some of the crises  that we've seen  um it doesn't take a rocket scientist to  see the connection for example between  the crisis which erupted in libya  and then the fallout which occurred  through the smuggling of arms through  the return of people through under  development as such in the region to see  how that then impacted on mali europeans  now have to step up to the plate um  of course the major concern here is the  american um uh shift or pivot to east  asia um this in turn means that um the  traditional security um buffer or or  provisions which the us gave to europe  in through nato and after the second  world war and during the cold war  is is steadily moving away from us and  this in turn means that europe must take  much more security responsibility  for its neighborhood  it's clear that the spending issue is a  real concern  but having said that  we can do a lot more together in europe  there are there's a lot more that some  of the key states from a security and  defense point of view can do to pool  their resources um in terms of their own  budgets but also in terms of joint  capability programs as such and uh at  the moment we're not doing as as much as  we possibly could do and that is that  the tasks that we face or the gravity of  the tasks that we face are not being met  seriously  with political leadership with the types  of capabilities that we need and also  the cooperation between member states it  does occur but it does occur at a really  low level at the moment  there have been over the last five to  six years a 5.5 billion reduction in the  amount of outlays which member state  governments  make on r d investments in the defense  sector which i think is quite a worrying  trend now on the other side one could  argue well yes we'll just let the  americans do all of the r d and simply  buy american products at the end of the  day well that's slightly more difficult  in the european uh political framework  of defence industrial cooperation as we  know  whether the member states can  come together in some form of a deal  again whether it be on a bilateral basis  or even small multilateral pockets of  rnd cooperation is is a question which  remains open  a lot of the focus i think leading up to  the defense council meeting has been on  such new technologies whether it be  drones or whether it be cyber security  which i think are really really  essential to the future technological  competitors competitiveness of europe  but also um to the uh military strength  of europe as well as it were through  such technologies that is maintaining a  comparative advantage over over  potential adversaries um whether or not  drones and cyber security however are  enough in themselves  is a different question  i think the focus has tended to move  away from some of the traditional  military assets  which we perhaps take for granted land  forces aircraft  and naval assets as well now it's true  there's a lot of good work going on on  air to where refuelling and so forth but  um i would like personally from the the  council meeting to see perhaps a lot  more emphasis on some of the traditional  assets which adversaries around the  world or potential adversaries are  developing a lot and they're investing a  lot of money in these traditional assets  i wouldn't expect too many conclusions i  mean that the main one i hope is that we  get some form of political declaration  on you know when you start to talk about  defence industrial cooperation when you  start to talk about military capability  development well the heads of state need  to answer one simple question and that  is what do you need them for  and i would really like personally to  hear a coherent answer to this  in a political declaration perhaps one  has to lower expectations but i do think  that there is some margin for maneuver  for the heads of state to put out a  clear signal  and response to the state of defense in  europe that starts with acknowledging  that there's a problem in the first  place  that would be really nice but second of  all apart from the small pet projects  which will probably be announced such as  the development of drones and so forth  we'd really like to see a kind of  long-term perspective of where the heads  of state  really want to move out of this crisis  of defence in europe  and that's not just the question of  projects that they can do together but  it's also about communicating the  necessity of defense the continued  necessity of defence in a post-cold war  and even post-9 11 world  you
 

